.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-JOB 1 "2026-10-01" "ShardPost 0.42.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-job \- plan, apply and watch maintenance jobs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl plan
.RB [ \-classes
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-mailbox
.IR mailbox ]
.RB [ \-uids
.IR list " | " \-deleted ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I kind account
.br
.B shardpostctl plan \-all
.RB [ \-classes
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-skip-idle ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I kind
.br
.B shardpostctl plan show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I plan-id
.br
.B shardpostctl apply
.RB [ \-wait ]
.RB [ \-poll
.IR duration ]
.RB [ \-idempotency-key
.IR key ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I plan-id
.br
.B shardpostctl check
.RB [ \-wait ]
.RB [ \-poll
.IR duration ]
.RB [ \-idempotency-key
.IR key ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl job list
.RB [ \-account
.IR account ]
.RB [ \-state
.IR state ]
.RB [ \-limit
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-after
.IR id ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B shardpostctl job show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I job-id
.br
.B shardpostctl job wait
.RB [ \-poll
.IR duration ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I job-id
.br
.B shardpostctl job cancel
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I job-id
.SH DESCRIPTION
Everything that runs long or cannot be undone runs as a job. Jobs live in
the shared admin store, survive restarts and are taken over by another
server if the one running them dies, continuing at their last checkpoint.
.PP
Dangerous work is done in two steps.
.B plan
computes what the job
.I would
do and stores that plan; nothing is changed yet.
.B apply
runs the plan, once, and only while it is valid
.RI ( jobs.plan_ttl_seconds ,
one hour by default). Before the first write the job compares the account
with the plan: if it drifted, the job ends without changing anything.
.PP
.B check
needs no plan: it runs the read-only index check on an account.
.PP
.B plan \-all
plans a batch: one kind run on every account of the account directory.
.SS plan
Computes what a job of that kind would do to the account and stores the
result. Nothing is changed. The plan carries a fingerprint of the state
it was computed from, and it expires
.RI ( jobs.plan_ttl_seconds ).
Role: the one the kind needs, see
.B Kinds
below.
.SS plan \-all
Plans a batch of the kind over every account of the account directory:
.BR index_check ,
.BR scrub ,
.B fts_rebuild
or
.BR attachment_backfill .
.B \-classes
selects the repair classes of a scrub as for one account;
.B \-skip-idle
(scrub only) leaves out the accounts that did not change since their
last batch scrub was complete and found nothing, for at most
.I scrub.interval_days
(7). The plan names the kind, its role, whether it locks the accounts,
the parallelism and the directory it walks; its account is empty and
its kind
.BR batch .
.B apply
runs it as one job: it walks the directory a page at a time and works on
.I jobs.batch_parallel
accounts at once
.RI ( scrub.parallel
for a scrub). The result counts the accounts done, skipped, gone (purged
meanwhile), failed and with findings, and names the first 50 that failed
or had findings; a batch with a failed account ends
.IR partially_failed .
A kind that locks the account
.RB ( fts_rebuild )
takes the lock per account and waits
.I jobs.settle_seconds
before it writes, as a single job does: while it holds an account, the
open IMAP and POP3 sessions of that account are disconnected at their
next command and deliveries wait, so such a batch disconnects every
account once, one after another. An account an import or another job
holds is tried again at the end, up to three times a lease period
apart, and then counted as failed. A scrub with
.B \-skip-idle
does not look at the private \eSeen marks of deleted public folders, nor
at debris that servers before 0.42.0 left without an intent, for the
accounts it skips; they wait until the account is scrubbed again, at
most
.I scrub.interval_days
later. An account with an open intent (what a crash left) is never
skipped. A cancel stops
the batch between two accounts. A server that takes the batch over
continues after the last page done.
.PP
A batch is refused (exit 65) until the account directory has completed
its backfill
.RB ( "shardpost index backfill accounts" ),
since accounts from before the directory would be missed. Role: the one
the kind needs, and the scope of all accounts: socket callers have it,
mutual-TLS clients only with
.I all_accounts
in their configuration. An export of every account is no batch kind:
the export streams one archive per request to the caller.
.SS plan show
Prints a stored plan again, by its id: what it would do, when it was
made and when it expires. Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS apply
Runs a stored plan, once. With
.B \-wait
the command stays until the job ends and exits with its outcome;
.B \-idempotency-key
makes a repeated call return the first job instead of starting a second
one. Role: the one the kind needs.
.SS check
Runs the read-only index check on an account without a plan, because it
changes nothing. Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS job list
The jobs of the cluster, oldest first, with their state. Narrow it with
.BR \-account ,
.B \-state
and
.BR \-limit ;
.B \-after
continues after the last id of the previous page. Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS job show
One job: kind, account, state, progress, attempts, the server running
it, and the error of a failed one. Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS job wait
Waits for a job to end and exits with its outcome, polling every
.B \-poll
(2s by default). This is what a script uses instead of a loop of
.BR "job show" .
Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS job cancel
Asks a job to stop. A job that has not started yet never starts; a
running one stops at its next safe point, and what it has already done
stays done. Role: the one the kind needs (for a batch the one of the
kind it runs, and the scope of all accounts).
.SS Kinds
.TP
.B index_check
Role
.BR diagnose .
Checks the index and reports findings. Repairs nothing. This is what
.B check
runs.
.TP
.B scrub
Role
.BR repair .
Checks and repairs.
.B \-classes
selects what may be repaired:
.IR deliveries ", " moves ", " retired ", " uncovered ", " entries ", "
.IR refs ", " orphans ", " unknown ", " quota .
Without the flag every class is repaired. Where the scrub looks for what
nothing points at is
.I scrub.orphan_source
in the servers' configuration:
.B listing
lists the account's namespace in both pools,
.B shadow
(the default) does that and also works out what the intents name,
reporting the differences in the field
.B Shadow
of the result,
.B intents
reads the intents only. With
.BR intents ,
.I orphans
covers the blobs of open intents and
.I unknown
the objects of abandoned creations and unfinished deletions, the marks
of deleted public folders and the METADATA of deleted mailboxes. The
result counts, besides the findings, the intents older than
.I scrub.intent_grace_seconds
.RB ( IntentsOpen )
and those removed
.RB ( IntentsDropped ).
.TP
.B quota_recalc
Role
.BR repair ,
locks the account. Counts the usage from the index again and stores it.
.TP
.B acl_reconcile
Role
.BR repair .
Writes missing share pointers at the grantees and removes pointers that
no longer carry rights.
.TP
.B fts_rebuild
Role
.BR repair ,
locks the account. Drops the full-text index (with its stored headers)
and builds it again from every message. When it completes it sets the
index version marker; without it, and while a rebuild runs or after
one stopped midway, IMAP substring search does not use the index and
checks every message of the mailbox. Run it for every account after an
upgrade that changes how text is indexed. It has no checkpoint: after a
takeover it starts over.
.TP
.B fts_dict_convert
Role
.BR repair .
Writes the substring dictionary of the full-text index again from the
posting lists and removes the objects of the layout used before 0.35.0.
It reads no message and does not lock the account; it stops when an
import or another job locks the account, and is then run again. The plan
shows how many objects of the old layout are left; the result counts the
terms by field, the dictionary entries written and the old objects
removed. Running it again is harmless, and it is also the repair for a
term that lost its dictionary entries (it is then found as a whole word
but not as a part of one). It has no checkpoint.
.TP
.B mailbox_name_convert
Role
.BR repair ,
locks the account. Renames mailboxes that IMAP clients without UTF-8
support created under ShardPost 0.35.2 or earlier, when their modified
UTF-7 names were stored as sent (for example
.I Entw&APw-rfe
for
.IR Entwürfe ),
to their UTF-8 names; children move with their parent. Subscriptions
without a mailbox are rewritten the same way. Only a name level that is
the exact modified UTF-7 form of another name changes;
.I R&D
stays. A rename whose new name exists already is reported as a
conflict, with its children, and nothing is renamed for it; a name that
is not valid UTF-8 is reported. The plan lists every change; check it
before applying, since a mailbox that a UTF-8 client really named
.I Entw&APw-rfe
would be renamed as well.
The usual conflict is a mailbox that delivery, Sieve or a JMAP client
created in UTF-8 next to the one an older IMAP client created in modified
UTF-7: merge the two by hand (move the messages, delete the empty one
with
.BR mailbox_delete )
and plan again. Sieve scripts, configured rules and client settings that
name the old form are not rewritten; a
.I fileinto
of the old name creates that mailbox again, so change them to the UTF-8
names.
.TP
.B attachment_backfill
Role
.BR repair .
Reads every message whose index record does not say whether it has an
attachment \(em messages stored before the record had the field \(em and
remembers the answer for JMAP (the
.I hasAttachment
filter of
.B Email/query
and the property of
.BR Email/get ).
It changes no message and no index record and does not lock the account;
it stops when an import or another job locks the account. The plan shows
how many such messages there are and how many are already remembered;
the result counts the messages read, the ones without data, the ones
that could not be read and the mailboxes that could not be listed, with
the first errors; neither a broken message nor a broken mailbox ends the
run. What a stopped run wrote stays, and running it
again reads only what is left. A JMAP query starts the same fill on its
own when it needs it; the job does it ahead of time.
.TP
.B mailbox_delete
Role
.BR delete ,
locks the account. Deletes the mailbox named by
.B \-mailbox
with its children. INBOX is refused.
.TP
.B expunge
Role
.BR delete .
Removes the messages named by
.B \-uids
or, with
.BR \-deleted ,
every message that carried
.I \eDeleted
when the plan was made. Only messages that still have the UID and GUID
from the plan are removed, at most 40000 per plan.
.TP
.B purge
Role
.BR delete ,
locks the account. Withdraws shares in both directions, deletes the
learning jobs and every object of the account, and finally removes it
from the account directory. Until that last step nothing can create the
account again. The identity itself (LDAP) is not touched: if the account
logs in again afterwards, it gets an empty INBOX. A purge that fails or
is cancelled after it deleted the data leaves the account
.B purging
in the directory; every later job that locks it, a second purge included,
then fails at once with "run shardpostctl account repair" (see
.BR shardpostctl-account (1)).
.SS The account lock
Jobs marked above as "locks the account" take the same lock an import
uses. While it is held, IMAP and POP3 answer "Mailbox maintenance in
progress", LMTP answers
.IR "451 4.2.1" ,
and other jobs and imports stay out. Sessions that were already open
notice within five seconds and are disconnected. The job waits
.I jobs.settle_seconds
(10 by default) after locking before it writes, so deliveries in flight
can finish.
.PP
If the account is already locked when a job starts, the job waits in
state
.I running
with the phase "waiting for the account lock" and tries again every lease
period, up to
.I jobs.plan_ttl_seconds
after it was created.
.SS States
.IR queued ", " running ", " succeeded ", " partially_failed ", " failed ", "
.IR cancel_requested ", " cancelled .
.B job cancel
stops a queued job at once and a running one at its next safe point; what
it already did is not rolled back.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-classes " list"
Repair classes of
.BR scrub ,
comma-separated.
.TP
.BI \-mailbox " mailbox"
Mailbox for
.B mailbox_delete
and
.BR expunge ,
by name or id.
.TP
.BI \-uids " list"
UIDs for
.BR expunge ,
comma-separated.
.TP
.B \-deleted
.B expunge
takes every message flagged
.IR \eDeleted .
.TP
.B \-all
.B plan
a batch of the kind over every account instead of one account.
.TP
.B \-skip-idle
.B plan \-all scrub
skips the accounts unchanged since their last clean batch scrub.
.TP
.B \-wait
Wait until the job ends. The exit code is then the job's outcome.
.TP
.BI \-poll " duration"
How often
.B \-wait
asks. Default:
.IR 2s .
.TP
.BI \-idempotency-key " key"
Repeating a request with the same key returns the job the first request
created instead of starting a second one. A different request with the
same key is refused. The key expires together with its job,
.I jobs.retention_days
after the job ended; then the same key starts a new job. Earlier
versions kept keys forever; those expire once
.B shardpost index backfill kv
has run. With
.I admin.kv_index: off
keys do not expire.
.TP
.BI \-account " account"
.TQ
.BI \-state " state"
.TQ
.BI \-limit " n"
.TQ
.BI \-after " id"
Narrow and page
.BR "job list" .
A client with an account scope must name an account.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
Is this account's index sound?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl check -wait user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
Delete a mailbox: look first, then act.
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -mailbox 'Archive/2019' mailbox_delete user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait 20260919T173044Z-38cd01e99e31
.RE
.fi
.PP
Empty the trash of an account for good:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -mailbox Trash -deleted expunge user@example.org
shardpostctl plan show <plan-id>          # how many, and which UIDs
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
The usage counter looks wrong after an incident:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan quota_recalc user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
Search finds nothing although the mail is there: rebuild the index. The
account is locked while this runs, so do it in a quiet window.
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan fts_rebuild user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
After the upgrade to 0.35.0, move an account to the new dictionary
layout. Search works before and after; the old objects take space until
this has run.
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan fts_dict_convert user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
After the upgrade from 0.35.2 or earlier, give mailboxes that older
IMAP clients created with modified UTF-7 names their UTF-8 names:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan mailbox_name_convert user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
Before the users of an account with older mail search by attachment
over JMAP, read its old messages once:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan attachment_backfill user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
Repair only what the check found, not everything:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -classes refs,orphans scrub user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check every account read-only and see which ones have findings:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -all index_check
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>        # done, failed, findings, finding_accounts
.RE
.fi
.PP
After an upgrade that changes how text is indexed, rebuild the index of
every account (each account is locked while its turn runs):
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -all fts_rebuild
shardpostctl apply <plan-id>
shardpostctl job wait <job-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
What the nightly scrub does (it is submitted by
.BR "shardpost scrub \-\-batch" ):
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -all -skip-idle scrub
shardpostctl apply <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
Offboarding: remove every trace of an account from the store (the
identity stays in LDAP):
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan purge user@example.org
shardpostctl plan show <plan-id>          # mailboxes, messages, shares
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
Retry an apply safely from a script, without risking a second run:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl apply -idempotency-key nightly-2026-09-20 <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.PP
What is running right now, and what happened to a job?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl job list -account user@example.org
shardpostctl job show 20260919T173049Z-a26a9aabfc44
shardpostctl job cancel 20260919T173049Z-a26a9aabfc44
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Without
.BR \-wait ,
the exit code only says whether the request was accepted. With
.B \-wait
(and for
.BR "job wait" )
it is the outcome of the job:
.TP
.B 0
The job succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The job partly failed; its errors are printed.
.TP
.B 65
The plan was already applied or has expired, or a batch was planned
before the account directory completed its backfill.
.TP
.B 67
No such job, plan or account.
.TP
.B 69
The job failed or was cancelled — including a job that ended because
the account had drifted from its plan, which is a failure of the job,
not of the submission.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role of this kind.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-account (1),
.BR shardpostctl-export (1),
.BR shardpostctl-mailbox (1),
.BR shardpostctl-message (1)
